e board acted on the 12/12 bids (p. 26).\n  Rows affected: #20, #22; #30, #31, #36.\n  What changes if answered differently: If 10/30 were Enforced, #22 is an on-time submission; if 12/12 were not Enforced, only the Rounds sheet changes.\n  Reviewer note: None\n---\n  Q: Q3\n  Question: First-round count: the background says six parties submitted indications of interest on 10/30, while the Reasons section says the Company 'received first round indications of interest from 5 bidder groups.' Which count should the ledger use?\n  Recommended answer: Six submitters, following the background's detailed chronology.\n  Why, with page: Six is stated in the operative narrative (p. 24); the reasons-page figure is a summary (p. 27) and the two are not reconciled anywhere in the filing.\n  Rows affected: #19, #21.\n  What changes if answered differently: With five submitters, #19's cohort becomes 1 and #21 becomes 15 − 5 = 10.\n  Reviewer note: None\n---\n  Q: Q4\n  Question: Bidder 3's two members are unnamed. Should they be recorded as the two unnamed finalists (Unnamed financial bidders 1 and 2), with Buyer Group and Bidder 2 continuing separately?\n  Recommended answer: Yes — of the four finalists, only those two can have combined; the filing never identifies them.\n  Why, with page: Four advanced at or above $80.00: Buyer Group, Bidder 2 and the two unnamed indications (p. 24); Buyer Group and Bidder 2 bid separately on 12/10 and 12/12 (pp. 25–26).\n  Rows affected: #17, #25, #29.\n  What changes if answered differently: If Bidder 3 included a different bidder, the count of independent bidders in the final stage changes (three bidding units either way).\n  Reviewer note: None\n---\n  Q: Q5\n  Question: One process or two? PetSmart's spring-2014 contacts with Industry Participant concerned PetSmart buying it, and Industry Participant was never admitted; the sell-side process ran from 08/13/2014 to signing. Should Number of processes stay 1?\n  Recommended answer: Yes — one process (Process 1); the spring episode is not a sell-side process.\n  Why, with page: C1: a target’s attempt to buy another company is not its sale process; the filing reports no abandoned sell-side attempt or restart (pp. 21–23).\n  Rows affected: Deal facts only; no ledger rows.\n  What changes if answered differently: If two processes, a Process terminated/Process restarted pair and spring contact rows would be added.\n  Reviewer note: None\n\nField || Value\nTarget || PetSmart, Inc., a Delaware corporation (NASDAQ: PETM); 99,455,151 shares outstanding on the 01/29/2015 record date.\nAcquirer || Argos Holdings Inc. (Parent) and Argos Merger Sub Inc., to be owned by the Buyer Group: funds advised by BC Partners, Inc., La Caisse de dépôt et placement du Québec, affiliates of GIC Special Investments Pte Ltd, affiliates of StepStone Group LP, and (after 12/12/2014) Longview Asset Management, LLC.\nAcquirer type || Financial — private-equity/sponsor consortium (with a Longview rollover); no strategic bidder reached the final round.\nAgreed price and consideration || $83.00 per share in cash; Longview contributes 3,012,050 shares (~$250 million) to Parent as rollover equity. Parent estimates ~$8.4 billion total funds required, with $6.95 billion debt commitments and up to $1.83 billion cash equity commitments.\nMerger agreement signed || 2014-12-14 00:00:00\nMerger announced || 2014-12-14 00:00:00\nFiling type and date || DEFM14A definitive proxy statement dated 02/02/2015 (special meeting 03/06/2015).\nBackground pages || 21–26\nInitiation || Activist-influenced — JANA Partners’ 13D and Longview’s public advocacy preceded the board’s 08/13/2014 decision to explore a sale; Industry Participant also re-approached J.P. Morgan on 08/07/2014.\nNumber of processes || 1\nAuction screen (C1) || Met — 15 independent prospective acquirers (all financial buyers) executed confidentiality and standstill agreements with the Company in the process.\nWhole-company bids || Yes — all indications and bids were for the whole company (e.g., Buyer Group $83.00 per share in cash).\nCurrency and units of bid prices || US dollars, per share of common stock (all bids were cash; October indications did not state form).\nTarget financial advisers || J.P. Morgan Securities LLC (sole financial advisor; engagement letter effective 08/21/2014).\nTarget legal advisers || Wachtell, Lipton, Rosen & Katz.\nAccount || PetSmart's board began reviewing alternatives in early 2014 and, after JANA Partners disclosed a 9.9% stake on July 3, 2014 and Longview publicly urged a sale, decided on August 13 to explore a sale and announced it on August 19. A private strategic suitor, Industry Participant, re-approached J.P. Morgan on August 7 but was told it would not be invited, because of antitrust-timing risk. During mid-August to end-October J.P. Morgan was contacted by 27 potential participants (three strategic, 24 financial); 15 financial buyers signed confidentiality and standstill agreements in the first week of October and received diligence and management presentations, and six submitted non-binding indications of interest due October 30. On November 3 the board advanced the four bidders at or above $80.00 to the final round; two of them combined as Bidder 3, which then valued the company at or below the market price and submitted nothing. Buyer Group bid $80.70 and Bidder 2 $80.35 on December 10; after the committee demanded improvement, their best-and-final bids on December 12 were $83.00 and $81.50, and the board approved the Buyer Group deal on December 13. The parties executed on December 14, 2014: $83.00 per share in cash, with Longview rolling 3,012,050 shares (~$250 million) into the buyer and no strategic acquirer reaching the final round.\n\nformats check:\nT2 MM/DD/YYYY | U2 MM/DD/YYYY | H15 General\nC2 MM/DD/YYYY\nB5 MM/DD/YYYY $83.00 per share in cash; Longview contributes 3,012,050 shares (~$250 million) to Parent as rollover equity. Parent estimates ~$8.4 billion total funds required, with $6.95 billion debt commitments and up to $1.83 billion cash equity commitments.\nB6 MM/DD/YYYY 2014-12-14 00:00:00\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Command exited with code 0."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /tmp/opencode/petsmart && python3 build.py && python3 - <<'EOF'\\nfrom openpyxl import load_workbook\\nwb = load_workbook('/home/uctpiaj/work/extraction/petsmart.xlsx')\\nws = wb['Rounds']\\nfor r in ws.iter_rows(values_only=True):\\n    print(r)\\nprint()\\nws = wb['Questions']\\nfor r in ws.iter_rows(values_only=True):\\n    print('---')\\n    for f, v in zip([c.value for c in ws[1]], r):\\n        print('  %s: %s' % (f, v))\\nprint()\\nws = wb['Deal facts']\\nfor r in ws.iter_rows(values_only=True):\\n    print(r[0], '||', r[1])\\nprint()\\nprint('formats check:')\\nws = wb['Deal ledger']\\nprint('T2', ws['T2'].number_format, '| U2', ws['U2'].number_format, '| H15', ws['H15'].number_format)\\nws = wb['Rounds']; print('C2', ws['C2'].number_format)\\nws = wb['Deal facts']; print('B5', ws['B5'].number_format, ws['B5'].value)\\nws = wb['Deal facts']; print('B6', ws['B6'].number_format, ws['B6'].value)\\nEOF\"}"},"time":{"start":1789825604729,"end":1789825607077}}}}
