{"type":"text","timestamp":1789825662820,"sessionID":"ses_f461eb89fffeglVev82zxa58vo","part":{"id":"prt_0b9ec2373001jL0Z7cPplP4AUA_text-0","sessionID":"ses_f461eb89fffeglVev82zxa58vo","messageID":"msg_0b9ec2373001jL0Z7cPplP4AUA","type":"text","text":"Done. Workbook delivered at `/home/uctpiaj/work/extraction/petsmart.xlsx` — four sheets in order (Deal ledger, Rounds, Questions, Deal facts), 41 ledger rows, real dates formatted MM/DD/YYYY, frozen headers, filters on, no merged cells. All quotes were machine-checked word-for-word against the filing, with printed pages (Background = pp. 21–26).\n\n## The sale, in short\n\nPetSmart's board had been reviewing alternatives since early 2014; after JANA Partners disclosed a 9.9% stake (07/03/2014) and Longview publicly urged a sale (07/07/2014), the board decided on 08/13/2014 to explore a sale and announced it on 08/19/2014. A private strategic suitor, \"Industry Participant,\" re-approached J.P. Morgan on 08/07/2014 but was told it would not be invited (antitrust-timing risk) — no strategic bidder ever reached the bidding stage. From mid-August to end-October J.P. Morgan was contacted by 27 potential participants (3 strategic, 24 financial); 15 financial buyers signed confidentiality and standstill agreements in the first week of October, did diligence and management presentations, and six submitted non-binding indications due 10/30 ($78.00 to $85.00). On 11/03 the board advanced the four bidders at or above $80.00 to the final round; two of them combined as Bidder 3. On 12/10 Buyer Group bid $80.70 and Bidder 2 $80.35; after the committee demanded improvement, the 12/12 best-and-final bids were $83.00 and $81.50, Bidder 3 having dropped out with a valuation at or below ~$78. The board approved on 12/13 and the parties signed on 12/14/2014: $83.00 cash per share, with Longview rolling 3,012,050 shares (~$250M) and voting 7,424,591 shares for the deal. One process, two rounds.\n\n## Open Questions (recommendations in the workbook)\n\n- **Q1 Round/process map** — keep one process; Round 1 opens with the first-week-of-October NDA wave (no dated outreach before it), Round 2 on 11/03, and the 12/10→12/12 improvement request as a deadline revision inside Round 2, not a third round.\n- **Q2 Deadline outcomes** — recommend *Late bids accepted* for 10/30 (Bidder 2 raised $78.00 to $81–84 post-deadline and was advanced); alternatives *Enforced*; Round 2 = *Extended; Enforced*.\n- **Q3 Count of first-round bids** — background says six submitters, the Reasons section says \"5 bidder groups\"; recommend six (detailed chronology wins).\n- **Q4 Bidder 3's members** — unnamed; recommend recording them as the two unnamed finalists (Unnamed financial bidders 1 and 2), since Buyer Group and Bidder 2 bid separately.\n- **Q5 One process or two** — recommend one; the spring-2014 Industry Participant talks were PetSmart buying another company, not its own sale process.\n\n## What I could not do\n\nThe filing never names Bidder 2, Bidders 3's members, the two below-$80 submitters, or the bidders behind the $80.00–$85.00 / undisclosed ≥$80 indications — descriptive names are used and the judgments flagged. Bidder 2's and Bidder 3's types are inferred as Financial (the 15 admitted buyers were all financial). The 5-vs-6 indication count cannot be reconciled internally, and a few timings (Bidder 3 formation, the 27 contacts) are windows rather than exact days.","time":{"start":1789825655678,"end":1789825662820}}}
